# Intel-only fleet.
#
# A single x86_64 kernel happily boots both vendors, and that is the right
# default when one image must cover a mixed fleet.  But it means every machine
# carries the other vendor's KVM implementation, IOMMU driver, EDAC driver,
# P-state driver, uncore PMUs and confidential-computing stack -- code that
# will never execute on it, and that is still reachable surface.
#
# Use this only if the fleet (or this SKU) is genuinely single-vendor.  The
# cost is a second image to qualify.
#
#   make PROFILE=hypervisor CPU=intel build
# CONFIG_KVM_AMD is not set
# CONFIG_KVM_AMD_SEV is not set
# CONFIG_CPU_SUP_AMD is not set
# CONFIG_AMD_IOMMU is not set
# CONFIG_EDAC_AMD64 is not set
# CONFIG_X86_AMD_PSTATE is not set
# CONFIG_PERF_EVENTS_AMD_UNCORE is not set
# CONFIG_PERF_EVENTS_AMD_POWER is not set
# CONFIG_SENSORS_K10TEMP is not set
# CONFIG_HW_RANDOM_AMD is not set
# The CCP is the AMD PSP mailbox; it exists here only to drive SEV.
# CONFIG_CRYPTO_DEV_CCP is not set
# CONFIG_CRYPTO_DEV_CCP_DD is not set
# CONFIG_CRYPTO_DEV_SP_PSP is not set

# Vendor-specific mitigations go with the vendor.  This is a feature, not a
# side effect: an Intel-only kernel has no reason to carry the mitigation for
# AMD's Speculative Return Stack Overflow, and carrying it means carrying its
# entry-path alternatives too.
# CONFIG_MITIGATION_SRSO is not set
# CONFIG_MITIGATION_IBPB_ENTRY is not set
